the family of a Chinese researcher who  vanished in Illinois is appealing to the  public for help today surveillance video  from June shows that ying-ying Jiang  getting into a black satin car on the  day that she disappeared investigators  believe that she is dead  the former graduate student Brent  Christianson has been charged with her  kidnapping Anna Werner spoke with  Jiang's family she is at the bus stop at  the University of Illinois where Jiang  was last seen alive and a good morning  good morning this bus stop behind me  this is the last place that Jiang was  seen alive that was over two months ago  her family has been waiting ever since  and they are heartbroken ying-ying  Jiang's mother sobbed throughout the  news conference where the family spoke  out for the first time since her  disappearance with each passing by day  the chances of finding a live decrease  Jiang's mother and brother arrived in  Illinois just a few days ago her  boyfriend Shaolin Ho says they all feel  helpless can you describe what this has  been like for the family for her mom and  dad woman every day is like torture for  us he says we live in agony every day 26  year old Jiang came from China to study  for her PhD at the University of  Illinois on June 9th surveillance  cameras captured her getting into this  black Saturn police say is owned by  Brent Christianson an FBI affidavit says  Christensen later admitted that he  offered jiang a ride and that she got in  his car but claims he let her out a few  blocks away the same affidavit also says  while under surveillance  he was recorded saying he brought jiang  back to his apartment and held her  against her will he faces one count of  kidnapping anthony bruno is one of  Christensen's defense attorneys our  client is presumed innocent his  presumption of innocence will remain  with him until this case is over Jiang's  family says they will stay until she is  found it is a Chinese tradition howe  says to be buried in your hometown  fallen leaves returns to its roots we  need to find her and take her home the  family has also sent a letter to  President Trump asking for  his help Christensen's trial is  scheduled to start in September but his  attorney says he'll be asking for a  delay in order to go through a large  amount of evidence provided by  prosecutors bill a lot of thanks
 

